# Normalization and quantifier elimination.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.

# The qelim command eliminates the non-temporal quantifiers from a formula.

qelim all X (X = 0)
qelim ex X (X != 0)

# As with every command, the argument may be a history position instead of a
# literal formula:

all X ex Y (X Y' = 0)
qelim %

# The normalize command, abbreviated n, is the central procedure. Applied to a
# formula it decides validity in the first order theory of countable atomless
# Boolean algebras: a valid formula normalizes to T and an unsatisfiable one to
# F. Untyped variables are exactly variables of that theory, which is why the
# formula below can be decided at all:

n all x ex y all v ex w x'y = 0 && v ^ w = 0

# And one that is not valid:

n all x ex y (x y = 0 && x' y != 0)

# The sat, unsat and valid commands answer the same kind of question directly,
# reporting T or F rather than returning a normalized formula:

valid all x (x x' = 0)
sat all x (x = 0)

# unsat asks the complementary question, so it answers T exactly where sat
# answers F -- not every element of an atomless Boolean algebra is 0:

unsat all x (x = 0)

# Applied to a term, normalize minimizes it, dropping the parts that the rest
# already covers. Everything below is subsumed by x:

n xyzvw | xyzv | xyz | xy | x

# The output is also canonical: two terms that denote the same Boolean function
# normalize to the same expression, however differently they were written. Note
# that the second command below reports the *same* history position as the first,
# because the result it produced was already stored there:

n xa ^ ya'
n a'y ^ ax

quit
